    "title": "<p>Diagnostic criteria for diffuse meningeal melanocytic neoplasms</p>",
    "legend": "",
    "source": "Wesseling P",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "<table class=\"MsoNormalTable\" style=\"width: 496.15pt; background: #DAEEF3; border-collapse: collapse;\" border=\"0\" width=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\">\r\n<tbody>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid; height: 10.2pt;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 496.15pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 10.2pt;\" valign=\"top\" width=\"662\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong><em>Essential:</em></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid; height: 10.2pt;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 496.15pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 10.2pt;\" valign=\"top\" width=\"662\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Diffuse or multifocal primary meningeal melanocytic neoplasm</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid; height: 10.2pt;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 496.15pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 10.2pt;\" valign=\"top\" width=\"662\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>AND</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid; height: 10.2pt;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 496.15pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 10.2pt;\" valign=\"top\" width=\"662\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span><em>For</em> <em>meningeal melanocytosis:</em> absence of CNS parenchyma invasion, absence of marked cytological atypia, absence of mitotic activity, and absence of necrosis</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span><em>For meningeal melanomatosis:</em> invasion of the CNS parenchyma and/or marked cytological atypia and/or mitotic activity and/or necrosis</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid; height: 10.2pt;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 496.15pt; background: #EFF6FC; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 10.2pt;\" valign=\"top\" width=\"662\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong><em>Desirable:</em></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid; height: 10.2pt;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 496.15pt; background: #EFF6FC; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 10.2pt;\" valign=\"top\" width=\"662\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">In children, meningeal melanocytosis/melanomatosis is often <em>NRAS</em>-mutant and rarely <em>BRAF</em>-mutant</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n</tbody>\r\n</table>",
